        <p>Read the last column top to bottom, because the pattern is the point of this section: <strong>the fewer hours you work, the more each hour keeps</strong>. That’s $45.48 at 20 hours a week against $42.23 at 40, a $3.25-an-hour gap on the same $54.00 rate. The reason is the bracket. None of a 20-hour year’s $40,060.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket, while a 25-hour year’s $54,100.00 of taxable income runs $3,700.00 into it, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three things in that table matter more than the headline. First, the 37.5-hour paid week, common in offices that pay for a 7.5-hour day, is $105,300.00 gross, which still beats a $105,000 salary by $300.00 gross and $211.05 of take-home. A 2.5-hour shorter week costs $7,020.00 gross and $4,938.57 of take-home. Second, the step from 30 to 40 hours is worth $28,080.00 gross and $19,754.28 of take-home ($1,646.19 a month). That is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Third, part-time keeps a bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 84.2% of gross, against 78.2% full-time, and a 20-hour week at $54 keeps exactly what a full-time $27-an-hour job keeps, $47,304.56, while a 25-hour week beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $140.70. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
